    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Off The Fence is the eleventh studio album by The James Hunter Six, released on January 16, 2026, via Easy Eye Sound, marking their first outing on the label founded by Dan Auerbach. Produced by Gabriel Roth with engineering by Anthony Masino and recorded in Riverside, California, the record features twelve tracks that distill Hunter's signature funk/soul sound, drawing from classic influences like Muscle Shoals R&B, doo-wop, jump blues, and Northern Soul. Standouts include the rumba-driven opener \"Two Birds One Stone,\" which evokes early Fame Records hits, and the title track alongside \"One for Ripley,\" channeling Brill Building songcraft, while \"Ain’t That A Trip\" showcases Hunter's virtuoso harmonica and a rare duet with longtime collaborator Van Morrison.\n\nHunter's gravelly, emotive voice—hailed by Van Morrison as one of British R&B's best-kept secrets—anchors the album's sincere blend of influences, from the ska-tinged soul-blues of \"Gun Shy\" (co-written with bassist Myles Weeks) to the primal funk of \"Trouble Comes Calling\" and the upbeat \"A Sure Thing.\" Over three decades, the Colchester native has evolved from busking London streets and backing Morrison on Days Like This (1996) to crafting original rhythm & soul gems, and Off The Fence cements his mastery without slipping into pastiche.\n\nCritics praise the album as a perfect amalgam of Hunter's songwriting prowess and timeless delivery, evoking forgotten 45s from a roadside jukebox—familiar yet vividly alive with his band's horn-sparked energy and subtle innovations. Tracks like \"Let Me Out Of This Love,\" \"Believe It When I See It,\" and \"Only A Fool\" highlight his ability to weave personal emotion into genre homage, making Off The Fence a vibrant addition to his catalog of honest, groove-laden soul.",
      "generated_at": "2026-01-17T09:33:12.456509",
      "model": "sonar",
